---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CompressLab from "../../components/CompressLab.astro";
import DedupLab from "../../components/DedupLab.astro";
import CryptLab from "../../components/CryptLab.astro";
import Excerpt from "../../components/Excerpt.astro";
import RoomNav from "../../components/RoomNav.astro";
import { gh } from "../../lib/openzfs";
import { room, roomUrl, roomNumber } from "../../lib/rooms";

const lang = "en";
const r = room("compression-dedup-encryption");
const Z = "module/zfs/zio.c";
const DDT = "module/zfs/ddt.c";
const DDTH = "include/sys/ddt.h";
const SPA = "include/sys/spa.h";
const CRYPT = "module/os/linux/zfs/zio_crypt.c";
const L = {
  compressData: gh("module/zfs/zio_compress.c", 148, 149),
  bpe: gh(SPA, 356, 357),
  roundup: gh(Z, 1783, 1789),
  minAlloc: gh("module/zfs/vdev.c", 428, 437),
  gcd: gh("module/zfs/vdev.c", 1523, 1536),
  asize: gh("module/zfs/vdev.c", 368, 370),
  holeBirth: gh(Z, 2107, 2115),
  setProps: gh(Z, 2116, 2132),
  dedupEmpty: gh("module/zfs/dmu.c", 2465, 2472),
  embeddedLayout: gh(SPA, 265, 323),
  syncPass: gh(Z, 1957, 1974),
  key: gh(DDTH, 95, 105),
  value: gh(DDTH, 150, 169),
  fill: gh(DDT, 724, 726),
  addref: gh(DDT, 880, 888),
  childWrite: gh(Z, 4110, 4114),
  extend: gh(Z, 3780, 3789),
  quotaComment: gh(DDT, 113, 127),
  overQuota: gh(DDT, 1136, 1163),
  logTxg: gh("module/zfs/ddt_log.c", 41),
  prune: gh(DDT, 129, 134),
  scrubDdt: gh(DDT, 180, 186),
  cryptDedup: gh(CRYPT, 147, 163),
  suites: gh(CRYPT, 39, 40),
  lens: gh("include/sys/zio.h", 129, 131),
  masterKey: gh("module/zfs/dsl_crypt.c", 37, 44),
  shareKey: gh("module/zfs/dsl_crypt.c", 60, 62),
  macros: gh(SPA, 452, 470),
  iv2: gh(SPA, 531, 538),
  encodeParams: gh(CRYPT, 751, 773),
  encodeMac: gh(CRYPT, 809, 827),
  noEmbed: gh(SPA, 320, 322),
  encryptRest: gh(Z, 5183, 5199),
  fold: gh("module/zfs/zio_checksum.c", 324, 341),
  scrubRaw: gh("module/zfs/dsl_scan.c", 4841),
  eacces: gh("module/zfs/dsl_crypt.c", 2818, 2823),
  rotate: gh(CRYPT, 187),
  dontCompress: gh(Z, 125),
  stages: gh("include/sys/zio_impl.h", 135, 144),
  logFlags: gh(DDT, 303, 310),
  quotaAuto: gh("module/zcommon/zpool_prop.c", 143, 145),
};
---

<Base
  lang={lang}
  roomId="compression-dedup-encryption"
  title="Compression, dedup, encryption · Uberblok"
  description="Three transforms in the ZFS write pipeline, read in the OpenZFS code: the compression limit and its four outcomes, the dedup table and what it costs, and the block pointer of an encrypted block, scrubbed without its key: interactive."
>
  <article class="wrap">
    <header class="room-head">
      <p class="kicker">Room {roomNumber(r.id)}</p>
      <h1>{r.title.en}</h1>
      <div class="prose problem">
        <p class="lead">
          Between the application and the disk, three features can change a block. Each saves something or protects
          something, and each has a price that the code pays on every write.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Compression</strong> saves space, but compressing costs CPU, and a block that shrinks by a few bytes
          saves nothing once it is rounded up to whole sectors. <strong>Dedup</strong> avoids writing a block that is
          already on disk, but to know that it must look every written block up in a table, keep that table on disk and
          update it. <strong>Encryption</strong> hides the data, but the pool must still be able to check, repair and
          send blocks for which it may not have the key. The code answers each problem with one rule, and this room
          takes the three rules one by one. In <a href={L.stages}>the write pipeline</a> they run in this order:
          compression, then encryption, then the checksum of what will be written, then the dedup lookup by that
          checksum.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="compress">
      <h2 id="compress"><span class="n">1</span> Compression: save an eighth, or store as is</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The compressor is given a limit: seven eighths of the block, rounded down to a whole number of sectors. If its
          output does not fit, the block is stored uncompressed. Two cases never reach the compressor or never reach
          the disk: a block of zeros becomes a hole, and an output of 112 bytes or less is folded into the block pointer
          itself. Choose the data, the block size, the sector size and the compression, and follow the branch that
          decides.
        </p>
      </div>
      <CompressLab lang={lang} />
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="dedup">
      <h2 id="dedup"><span class="n">2</span> Dedup: a table looked up on every write</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          With <code>dedup=on</code>, every block written is looked up in the dedup table (DDT) by its checksum. If the
          table already has it, the new block pointer is filled with the existing copy’s address and a reference count
          goes up: nothing is written. If not, the block is written and a new entry records where. Write blocks, write
          the same one again, then put the table over its quota.
        </p>
      </div>
      <DedupLab lang={lang} />
      <div class="prose after cost">
        <p>
          <strong>What it costs.</strong> The figure counts one DDT lookup per write, hit or miss: a dataset with
          <code>dedup=on</code> pays it for every block, not only for duplicates. Each entry is a key of
          <a href={L.key}>40 bytes</a> (the 256-bit checksum and one word of sizes and flags) and a value of
          <a href={L.value}>256 bytes in the traditional format, 72 in the newer flat one</a>. The table lives on disk as
          ZAP objects, and the code says why updating it is expensive: “each entry only takes up a tiny portion of a ZAP
          leaf node, and so required reading the whole node, updating the entry, and writing it back. On busy pools,
          this could add serious IO and memory overheads.” The <code>fast_dedup</code> feature answers with a log:
          modified entries are appended to an on-disk log and kept in memory, and flushed to the ZAP a little every
          txg. Only tables created with the feature <a href={L.logFlags}>get the log</a>; a table created before it
          keeps the rewrite of a ZAP leaf per txg. A <code>dedup_table_quota</code> can cap the table’s size on disk;
          over it, new blocks are simply not deduplicated. Its default, <code>auto</code>, <a href={L.quotaAuto}>sets no
          size</a> and <a href={L.overQuota}>caps the table only on a pool with a dedup or special vdev</a>: on a pool
          of plain disks, the table grows with the data. The code gives no figure for memory per terabyte or for a dedup ratio: those depend on the
          data, and this room does not invent them.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="crypt">
      <h2 id="crypt"><span class="n">3</span> Encryption: secrets in the block pointer, checksums in clear</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          An encrypted block needs a salt, an IV and a MAC, and they travel where the block is referenced: in its block
          pointer, in the place of the third copy’s address and of half the checksum. The other half of the checksum is
          computed over the ciphertext, so the pool can check every block without the key. Compare the words of four
          kinds of block pointer, then unload the key of an encrypted file and scrub it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <CryptLab lang={lang} />
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="depths">
      <h2 id="depths">The idea, at three depths</h2>

      <div class="depth">
        <h3>In one sentence</h3>
        <p class="lead prose">
          Compression keeps a block compressed only if it saves at least an eighth, counted in whole sectors; dedup replaces a
          write with a reference when the block’s checksum is already in its table, at the price of a lookup on every
          write; encryption puts its salt, IV and MAC in the block pointer and leaves a truncated checksum of the
          ciphertext, so scrub needs no key.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="depth">
        <h3>In one paragraph</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          In <code>zio_write_compress</code>, an all-zero block becomes a hole; otherwise the compressor gets a limit of
          seven eighths of the block, snapped down to the sector, and an output that does not fit leaves the block
          uncompressed; an output of at most 112 bytes goes into the block pointer, which then holds data instead of
          addresses; anything else is rounded up to whole sectors. Then, in an encrypted dataset, encryption uses the
          third DVA for the salt and the start of the IV, the top of the fill count for the rest of the IV, and half of
          the checksum for the MAC; only level-0 blocks of encrypted types are encrypted. The checksum comes next,
          computed over what will be written, ciphertext included, and scrub, which reads blocks raw, checks its
          remaining 128 bits. Last, with dedup, <code>zio_ddt_write</code> looks that checksum up in the DDT: a hit
          copies the entry’s DVA into the new block pointer and adds a reference, with no write; a miss writes the block
          and records its DVA in a new entry; a table over its quota refuses new entries, and the block is written
          without its D bit.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="depth">
        <h3>In detail</h3>
        <div class="prose">
          <p><strong>The limit.</strong> Before compressing, the write path asks how big the output may be.</p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={Z}
          from={1791}
          to={1809}
          symbol="zio_get_compression_max_size"
          notes={[
            { from: 1797, to: 1798, text: "Seven eighths of the source: at least 12.5% must be saved, a “legacy value”, says the comment." },
            { from: 1800, to: 1802, text: "ZLE, which only compresses runs of zeros, is exempt from what follows." },
            { from: 1804, text: `Rounded down to a multiple of <code>spa_gcd_alloc</code>, which for a pool of plain disks of one ashift is <a href="${L.minAlloc}">the sector</a> (<a href="${L.gcd}">the greatest common divisor of the vdevs’ allocation sizes</a>): a saving smaller than a sector saves nothing.` },
            { from: 1806, to: 1807, text: `If that leaves less than one sector, the only output worth having is one that fits in a block pointer: <code>BPE_PAYLOAD_SIZE</code>, <a href="${L.bpe}">14 words of 8 bytes</a>, 112 bytes.` },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            Seven eighths of 128 KiB is 112 KiB, already a multiple of 512 bytes, 4 KiB and 8 KiB: for the default record
            size the snap changes nothing. It matters for small blocks: at ashift 12, seven eighths of 8 KiB snaps down to
            4 KiB, and seven eighths of 4 KiB snaps to nothing, so a 4 KiB block on 4 KiB sectors is compressed only if it
            shrinks to 112 bytes. The figure’s table shows the same data at the three sector sizes.
          </p>
          <p><strong>The four outcomes.</strong></p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={Z}
          from={1982}
          to={2017}
          symbol="zio_write_compress"
          notes={[
            { from: 1983, to: 1984, text: `None of this runs with compression off: a block of zeros is then written like any other, unless dedup is on, which <a href="${L.dedupEmpty}">turns zero detection on</a> because “storing many references to an all zeros block in the dedup table would be expensive”.` },
            { from: 1986, to: 1987, text: "All zeros: PSIZE 0, which becomes a hole below." },
            { from: 1991, to: 1995, text: `The compressor runs with the limit as its output size; <a href="${L.compressData}"><code>zio_compress_data</code> returns the source size</a> when the output does not fit.` },
            { from: 1996, to: 1997, text: "A hole: compression is marked off, and PSIZE stays 0." },
            { from: 1998, to: 2001, text: "An output that saved nothing: compression is off for this block, and it is stored at its logical size." },
            { from: 2002, to: 2017, text: `At most 112 bytes, not encrypted, at level 0, of a type without a fill count, with <code>embedded_data</code> enabled: the data is encoded into the block pointer, whose <a href="${L.embeddedLayout}">words then hold payload</a>, and the write stops there, with no allocation.` },
          ]}
        />
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={Z}
          from={2018}
          to={2039}
          symbol="zio_write_compress"
          notes={[
            { from: 2027, to: 2028, text: `<code>zio_roundup_alloc_size</code> <a href="${L.roundup}">rounds up to the minimum allocation, or to a multiple of the sector above it</a>.` },
            { from: 2029, to: 2032, text: "If that is not smaller than the block, it is stored uncompressed. The snapped limit makes this rare; ZLE, unsnapped, reaches it, and so does a 112-byte output of an encrypted block, which cannot be embedded." },
            { from: 2034, to: 2035, text: `The tail of the last sector is zeroed and PSIZE is the rounded size, so that “the compressed size of the BP (and thus compressratio property) are correct”. A hole <a href="${L.holeBirth}">allocates nothing; if the block existed before, it keeps its size, type and level, and is born in this txg (<code>hole_birth</code>)</a>, so an incremental send sees it; other blocks <a href="${L.setProps}">get PSIZE, compression, checksum and the D bit</a>.` },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            On a single disk, the space allocated is PSIZE <a href={L.asize}>rounded up to the sector</a>: a block is
            charged whole sectors, compressed or not, which is why the limit counts in sectors.
          </p>
          <p><strong>Dedup: the lookup.</strong> A block with the D bit goes through <code>zio_ddt_write</code>.</p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={Z}
          from={3816}
          to={3832}
          symbol="zio_ddt_write"
          notes={[
            { from: 3823, text: "Every dedup write looks its block pointer up, under the DDT’s lock." },
            { from: 3824, to: 3832, text: "NULL: the table is over its quota. The D bit is cleared and the block goes through the ordinary write pipeline, not deduplicated." },
          ]}
        />
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={DDT}
          from={1374}
          to={1387}
          symbol="ddt_lookup"
          notes={[
            { from: 1376, to: 1384, text: `NULL comes from here: the key is in no table (a block never seen) and <a href="${L.overQuota}"><code>ddt_over_quota</code></a> says the table is too big. A key that is found is still returned: over quota, a duplicate is still deduplicated; <a href="${L.quotaComment}">only new entries are refused</a>.` },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p><strong>A hit.</strong> An entry with enough DVAs for the block’s <code>copies</code>:</p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={Z}
          from={3958}
          to={3987}
          symbol="zio_ddt_write"
          notes={[
            { from: 3962, text: "The entry holds as many DVAs as the write needs." },
            { from: 3971, to: 3981, text: "A rewrite keeps its original birth if that gives the same block pointer: then nothing at all changes." },
            { from: 3983, to: 3986, text: `<code>ddt_bp_fill</code> copies the entry’s DVAs into the new block pointer, <a href="${L.fill}">with the current txg as logical birth and the entry’s as physical birth</a>; <a href="${L.addref}"><code>ddt_phys_addref</code></a> adds a reference; the function returns with no write I/O.` },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            The two births are why the figure shows “born 102, physical 100”: the data was written in txg 100, and the
            block pointer created in txg 102. The <a href={roomUrl(lang, "send-receive")}>send / receive room</a> sends
            by logical birth, so a block deduplicated after a snapshot is still sent.
          </p>
          <p><strong>A miss.</strong> A new entry has no DVA: the hit branch is not taken, and a child write is <a href={L.childWrite}>issued with the entry attached</a>.</p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={Z}
          from={4156}
          to={4167}
          symbol="zio_ddt_write"
          notes={[
            { from: 4161, text: "The new entry’s refcount goes from 0 to 1 now, before the write completes; it is undone if the write fails." },
            { from: 4165, text: `The child write runs; when it has its DVA, <a href="${L.extend}"><code>zio_ddt_child_write_ready</code> copies it into the entry</a>, for the next hit.` },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p><strong>The table on disk.</strong> The entries modified in a txg must reach the DDT’s storage objects.</p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={DDT}
          from={136}
          to={156}
          symbol="DDT: the dedup log"
          notes={[
            { from: 138, to: 142, text: "Without the log, every modified entry was written back at the end of its txg, and an entry is a tiny part of a ZAP leaf: reading the whole leaf, updating one entry, writing the leaf back." },
            { from: 144, to: 150, text: `With <code>fast_dedup</code>, on a table created with it (<a href="${L.logFlags}">only those get <code>DDT_FLAG_LOG</code></a>), modified entries go to an in-memory log and are appended to an on-disk log, read back only at import. By default the logs are swapped after <a href="${L.logTxg}">8 txgs at most</a>.` },
            { from: 152, to: 156, text: "Some of the log is flushed to the ZAP every txg, as much as keeps up without hurting throughput, as the <code>zfs_dedup_log_*</code> tunables decide, “and not using too much memory”. The comment gives no number here." },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            Two more mechanisms complete the picture, in the same comment: <a href={L.prune}>pruning</a> removes old
            entries that were never duplicated, to make room under the quota, and scrub <a href={L.scrubDdt}>walks the DDT
            first</a>, so that a block referenced many times is scrubbed once.
          </p>
          <p><strong>The encrypted block pointer.</strong></p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={SPA}
          from={188}
          to={225}
          symbol="blkptr_t, encrypted"
          notes={[
            { from: 194, to: 201, text: "The first two DVAs, as in any block pointer: an encrypted block keeps at most two copies." },
            { from: 202, to: 205, text: `The third DVA’s two words hold the salt and IV1, the first 64 bits of the 96-bit IV (<a href="${L.encodeParams}"><code>zio_crypt_encode_params_bp</code></a>).` },
            { from: 216, text: `IV2, the last 32 bits of the IV, in the <a href="${L.iv2}">upper half of the fill count</a>, which an encrypted level-0 block does not need.` },
            { from: 218, to: 225, text: `128 bits of checksum, then the 128-bit MAC (<a href="${L.encodeMac}"><code>zio_crypt_encode_mac_bp</code></a>).` },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            The X bit, bit 61 of word 6, has <a href={L.macros}>three meanings</a>: on a level-0 block of an encrypted type
            it means encrypted; on a level-0 block of an unencrypted type, authenticated; on an indirect block, that the
            MAC words hold a checksum of the MACs below. An embedded block pointer <a href={L.noEmbed}>cannot be
            encrypted</a>: it has no words left for salt, IV and MAC. The cipher is <a href={L.suites}>AES-GCM or AES-CCM,
            with 128, 192 or 256-bit keys</a>, authentication SHA512-HMAC, with an <a href={L.lens}>8-byte salt, a 12-byte
            IV and a 16-byte MAC</a>.
          </p>
          <p><strong>What each kind of block gets.</strong></p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={Z}
          from={5123}
          to={5153}
          symbol="zio_encrypt"
          notes={[
            { from: 5123, to: 5131, text: "An indirect block is not encrypted: its block pointer gets, in the MAC words, a checksum of the MACs of the blocks it points to." },
            { from: 5137, to: 5144, text: "The objset block carries its two MACs inside itself." },
            { from: 5146, to: 5153, text: `A level-0 block of an unencrypted type is only authenticated: a MAC, the data in clear. <a href="${L.encryptRest}">What is left is encrypted</a>, and salt, IV and MAC are encoded in its block pointer.` },
          ]}
        />
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path="module/zfs/dmu.c"
          from={2511}
          to={2535}
          symbol="dmu_write_policy"
          notes={[
            { from: 2523, to: 2526, text: "In an encrypted dataset, an encrypted type keeps at most two copies, since the third DVA holds salt and IV." },
            { from: 2527, to: 2529, text: "Blocks of unencrypted types are not deduplicated: the DDT does not record object types." },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p><strong>What stays in clear, and why scrub needs no key.</strong></p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={CRYPT}
          from={85}
          to={120}
          symbol="zio_crypt.c, block comment"
          notes={[
            { from: 89, to: 91, text: "The MAC takes the second half of the checksum, “leaving the first 128 bits for a truncated regular checksum of the ciphertext which can be used for scrubbing”." },
            { from: 94, to: 100, text: "Object types that must stay readable, the master node among them, are only authenticated, and can keep up to three copies." },
            { from: 115, to: 120, text: "The core of every dnode stays in plaintext “for scrubbing and claiming”; only the bonus buffers are encrypted." },
          ]}
        />
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path="module/zfs/zio_checksum.c"
          from={508}
          to={526}
          symbol="zio_checksum_error_impl"
          notes={[
            { from: 515, to: 516, text: "A block pointer with the X bit, except an objset block." },
            { from: 517, to: 520, text: `For a checksum not made for dedup (fletcher-4, for instance), words 2 and 3 are folded into 0 and 1, <a href="${L.fold}">as the writer did</a>.` },
            { from: 522, to: 525, text: "Words 2 and 3 are ignored on both sides: the MAC is left to decryption." },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            Scrub reads with <a href={L.scrubRaw}><code>ZIO_FLAG_RAW</code></a>, which skips decryption, so this check is
            all it does: the figure’s scrub verifies every block with the key unloaded, and catches a flipped bit. A read
            that must decrypt <a href={L.eacces}>looks the key up and fails with <code>EACCES</code></a> when it is not
            loaded. The key itself is a randomly generated master key, <a href={L.masterKey}>stored on disk
            encrypted with the user’s wrapping key</a>, so changing the passphrase re-encrypts no data; snapshots and
            clones <a href={L.shareKey}>share it</a>. Encrypted blocks can be deduplicated only if they have the same
            ciphertext: with dedup, their salt and IV <a href={L.cryptDedup}>come from an HMAC of the plaintext</a> instead of being random, and dedup works
            only within a clone family.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section simplified" aria-labelledby="simplified">
      <h2 id="simplified">What the figures leave out</h2>
      <p class="prose muted">
        The compressor’s output is an input of the figure, not computed by a real compressor: the code gives no ratio,
        and the presets are examples. LZ4 stands for every compressor but ZLE; they all follow the same limit. Compression
        levels are not shown, nor the rule that <a href={L.syncPass}>stops compressing</a> rewrites from
        <a href={L.dontCompress}>sync pass 8</a> on. The pool is plain disks of one ashift, where the minimum allocation and
        the sector are the same; raidz and mixed pools are not. Dedup writes one block per txg with
        <code>copies=1</code>; dedup verify, collisions, more copies, the partial-DVA extension of fast dedup and the
        log are told, not animated; DVAs are made up. The figure’s ciphertext, salt, IV and MAC are stand-ins, not AES;
        the checksum is fletcher-4 of the figure’s bytes. Key rotation, which takes a new salt after
        <a href={L.rotate}>400,000,000 blocks</a>, ZIL encryption and the objset MACs are not shown, and neither is any
        throughput, memory or ratio figure: the code does not give one.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
  <RoomNav lang={lang} roomId="compression-dedup-encryption" />
</Base>

